          "question": "What should I use instead for the knees?",
          "answer": "I can't name a specific replacement for ibuprofen from these sources, and I won't suggest a product or dose. Here is what the osteoarthritis guidance does say.\n\n**Exercise comes first.** The osteoarthritis summary (Osteoarthritis) describes exercise as the first-line treatment for knee osteoarthritis. It says that resting the joint can make it worse because muscle weakens. The lifestyle reference (Exercising with osteoarthritis) adds that supportive, cushioned footwear is generally suggested, and that a walking stick used on the side opposite the painful knee can reduce load on the joint.\n\n**Other options are listed as add-ons to exercise.** The rheumatology guidance (Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management) lists these, without saying which is right for you:\n- Topical anti-inflammatory creams or gels, which it calls the preferred first drug option for knee osteoarthritis, with lower whole-body exposure than tablets.\n- Oral anti-inflammatory tablets such as ibuprofen, used at the lowest effective dose for the shortest time, after stomach, heart and kidney risks are assessed.\n- Steroid injections into the joint, for short-term relief during a flare.\n- Duloxetine, for widespread pain or when other options do not work well enough.\n\nThe same guidance says opioids are not recommended for this condition, and that glucosamine and chondroitin have no clinically meaningful effect.\n\nThe sources do not say how the topical option would interact with your reflux or whether it is suitable for you. Please ask your doctor or pharmacist which option fits your reflux and your knees. Do not stop or change any medicine on your own first.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
